3. Algorithm
1.	Start.
2.	Read the number of emergency stations \(n\) and road connections \(e\).
3.	Create an adjacency list to represent the graph.
4.	Read all road connections and add each connection to the adjacency list.
5.	Accept the starting vertex from the user.
6.	Check whether the starting vertex is valid.
7.	Initialize all vertices as unvisited and set their BFS levels to \(-1\).
8.	Mark the starting vertex as visited, set its level to \(0\), and insert it into the queue.
9.	While the queue is not empty:
o	Remove the front vertex.
o	Add it to the traversal order.
o	Visit each unvisited neighbouring vertex.
o	Mark each neighbour as visited, set its level to the current vertex's level plus one, and insert it into the queue.
10.	Display the BFS traversal order.
11.	Display all reachable vertices and identify unreachable vertices.
12.	Count and display the number of reachable vertices.
13.	Display the BFS level of each reachable vertex.
14.	Stop.
